  "worked_solution": "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $f(2)=2(2)-3=1,$ so $f(2)$ exists. $\u2713$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2x+3) \\\\  & =-2(2)+3 \\\\  & =-1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}(2x-3) \\\\  & =2(2)-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist. $\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
